 },
 {
  "path": "/politics/2026/nov/13/nigel-farage-personal-security-costs-reform-uk",
  "kind": "article",
  "ok": true,
  "issues": [],
  "tone": {
   "ok": false,
   "issues": [
    "The headline 'Labour and Tories demand Farage explain Reform UK's funding of his security' matches the brief's own explicit failing pattern (cf. 'Rail unions call for more staff and policing as reported assaults hit record high'): grammatically the subject is Labour and the Tories, but the content is still the funding discrepancy itself, merely wrapped in a 'calls for explanation' frame, which is a cosmetic reframing of the original's angle rather than a genuine upside (rules 1 and 2).",
    "The rewrite moves the piece's harshest lines — Phillipson's 'Reform are up to their necks in sleaze and scandal... not fit for office' and the Tory spokesperson's 'fishy bung' — up to immediately follow the standfirst, front-loading attack rather than any upside and leaving the reader with a stronger sense of political decline than the original gave, contrary to rule 3.",
    "The original offers no genuine corrective action (Reform declines to comment, nothing is fixed, reviewed or paid back), so the strongest honest upside here is a weak 'call for action' worth at most upside 1, which the brief says 'is not enough to build a headline that leaves the reader feeling the world is being made better' — the story should have been scored and written plainly rather than led on the cross-party demand framing."
   ]
  }
